+  movdqa [rcx],xmm1; movdqa [rcx+r8-0x10],xmm2; ret. (standard overlapping copy)
+- 0x19e940e = alignment prologue: mov r9,rcx; and r9,0xf; sub r9,0x10 (r9 = NEGATIVE
+  align offset, e.g. -8); sub rcx,r9; sub rdx,r9; add r8,r9. -> r9=-8 is INTENTIONAL
+  alignment math, NOT corruption. Correct translation.
+- 0x19e9430 = the 16-byte MOVDQU chunk loop (fault site): movdqu xmm1,[rdx];
+  movdqu xmm2,[rdx+0x10]; ...  (reads source rdx=0x320ffff7c)
+
+=> The memcpy codegen/prologue is CORRECT. r9=-8 is intentional. The fault is that
+the memcpy SOURCE rdx=0x320ffff7c is UNMAPPED. rax=0x32023c018, rdi=0x320213740 are
+in a mapped heap range (0x3202xxxxx) but rdx=0x320ffff7c is in 0x320fxxxxx (a
+different/unmapped sub-range). So the CALLER passed memcpy a source pointer
+(0x320ffff7c) that should be a valid Unity buffer but is NOT mapped under HB/Wine.
+
+CONCLUSION: the frontier fault is NOT coherence, NOT memcpy codegen, NOT the 128-bit
+load path. It is a HEAP/ALLOCATION DIScrepancy: Unity believes it has a buffer at
+0x320ffff7c, but HB/Wine's virtual-memory/heap layer has NOT mapped that address.
+This is the "Unity allocator fault" class the journal chased. The fix is in the
+Wine/HB memory-allocation / virtual-memory layer (why an address Unity allocates /
+expects is not backed), NOT in the JIT codegen.
+
+NEXT: trace the allocation of 0x320ffff7c — which Unity allocator call produced it
