+map/commit it. Tools: MACRUNNER_HB_TRACE_HEAP_IMPORT / MACRUNNER_HB_TRACE_IMAGE_API
+/ a watch on the 0x320fxxxxx range, or compare Wine's VMA map vs Unity's expectation
+at the fault. Then fix the allocation/commit path -> source mapped -> memcpy
+succeeds -> HK proceeds past rva=0x19e9430 -> toward CreateSwapChain -> c000 -> pixel.
+
+### DEEPEST PIN: CRT stdio stream with NULL buffer (bufdesc=0) -> memcpy fault
+
+The memcpy DEST rcx=0x320ffffa0 is a CRT stdio STREAM (macrunner-hb-crt-forensics
+tracer, line 26121): stream=0x320ffffa0 ch=0x00 ... bufdesc=0x0 cur=0x0 end=0x0.
+The stream has NO buffer (bufdesc=0, cur=0, end=0 = uninitialized/NULL). The
+memcpy SOURCE rdx=0x320ffff7c is 0x24 bytes below the stream object -> a derived
+buffer address that is UNMAPPED because the stream buffer was never allocated.
+
+=> The frontier fault (MOVDQU at rva=0x19e9430, status c000007b runtime) is a CRT
+stdio stream-buffer SETUP failure: a stream's buffer (bufdesc/cur/end) is NULL,
+so a stdio memcpy reads from an unmapped derived address and faults. NOT
+coherence, NOT memcpy codegen, NOT the 128-bit load path, NOT a generic heap
+discrepancy — specifically a CRT stream with no buffer.
+
+FIX TARGET: why is the CRT stream's bufdesc=0? Investigate the CRT (ucrtbase/
+msvcrt) stream-init / buffer-alloc translation under HB — the stream buffer
+alloc (malloc/HeapAlloc for the stream's internal buffer) either failed silently
+or the stream-init code was mis-translated so bufdesc stays 0. Tools:
+macrunner-hb-crt-forensics is already in place; add the stream-init call trace
+(the fls/stream init, __acrt_initialize / _ioinit / _initterm) and the buffer
+alloc. Once bufdesc is set (buffer mapped), the stdio memcpy reads from a mapped
+buffer -> no fault -> HK proceeds past 0x19e9430 -> CreateSwapChain -> c000 -> pixel.
+
+### Refinement: the STREAM POINTER itself is unmapped -> upstream bad ptr; need call stack
+
+crt-forensics (line 26121) reads the stream at rcx=0x320ffffa0 via hb_memory_read
+and got ALL ZERO (p1=0, table=0, bufdesc=0, cur=0, end=0, cur_ch=0). The stream
+object lives in the SAME 0x320fxxxxx range as the unmapped source rdx=0x320ffff7c.
+=> the whole 0x320fxxxxx range is likely UNMAPPED, i.e. the STREAM POINTER rcx
+itself is invalid (points to unmapped memory), not just its buffer. So a stdio
+function was CALLED with an invalid stream pointer (rcx=0x320ffffa0) and source
+rdx=0x320ffff7c — both in the unmapped 0x320fxxxxx range.
+
+The r15-ring (16 blocks) is entirely INSIDE memcpy (the loop iterated many times
+before faulting), so it does NOT reach memcpy's CALLER. To find who passed the bad
+stream pointer, need the GUEST CALL STACK at the fault (return addresses up the
+frame from rsp=0x1168ee458 / rbp).
